WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.280
Hi today, we're doing
something a little different.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.460
Steve Kaufmann here, of course,
along with my son, Mark.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.410
And for those of you, for those of you
who don't know, mark is the CEO of LingQ.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.980
He's the one who decides
what we're going to do.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.300
He organizes people on five
continents that work for LingQ, you

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.060
know,  programmers, designers and...

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.400
a great team of people and so forth.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:28.230
And, uh, so I'm very excited today
to be able to interview Mark or he

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:35.070
interviews me and we talk about LingQ
5.0, which has finally finally launched.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.160
It was launched on the mobile
app a little while ago and now

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.960
it's launched on the browser.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.310
Mark, could you maybe introduce some
of the things that are in LingQ 5.0?

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.230
Oh, sure.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:54.390
I mean, obviously we just finally
pushed our web version a few days ago.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:59.220
And so it's obviously, there's been a
lot of, uh, discussion on the forum.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.970
We had the iOS of course
version has been available for

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.530
probably a month and a half now.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.770
Android also has been released more or
less recently to most users, uh, in the,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.570
in the, at the same time as the web app.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.759
So, um, there are a lot
of comments on the forum.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.080
Some people really like it.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.550
Some people are having trouble, uh,
getting used to it, uh, as always

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.600
happens when, when changes are
made to any, any software product.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:34.410
Um, obviously we're aware that, um, for
those people who really liked the way link

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.570
4.0 worked, uh...some love the change.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.229
Others are wondering why we made changes.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:47.160
And so we thought it'd be a good
opportunity to explain why we did

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.300
some of the things we did and what
some of the benefits are to version

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:58.080
five that, that probably may not all
be, um, obvious right at first blush.

00:01:58.589 --> 00:02:03.750
Well, what, what I'd like to start in
with is I am very enthusiastic about

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.220
LingQ 5.0, and I'm more of a user.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:09.690
Mark is the one who put a lot of
thought together with his team

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.009
and do what we should have there.

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:18.209
What I see with LingQ 5.0 is it's sort of
like a new start for LingQ it's, it's a

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:25.109
more exciting way of getting at content,
better organized, um, more motivating.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.280
A platform which allows LingQ
to develop new functions.

00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:32.655
It's it's a totally new product.

00:02:32.954 --> 00:02:34.994
That's not immediately obvious to people.

00:02:35.114 --> 00:02:39.734
So, um, I'm excited about it, but I think
it would be worthwhile to hear from Mark

00:02:39.975 --> 00:02:44.024
in some detail about some of the things
that are there, but bear in mind that

00:02:44.024 --> 00:02:45.844
you have to get in there and use them.

00:02:46.204 --> 00:02:51.885
Overcome the strangeness of new features
in order to fully appreciate the extent

00:02:51.885 --> 00:02:57.315
to which this is really a much more
powerful way of learning languages

00:02:57.495 --> 00:02:59.475
without realizing that you're learning.

00:02:59.535 --> 00:03:01.845
And I, that's what I
take away from LingQ 5.0.

00:03:03.495 --> 00:03:04.875
Uh, absolutely.

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:13.050
And I think when we started to, to
make LingQ 5.0 in effect, uh, worth

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:18.885
pointing out that version five, unlike
version four is, is, um, the core

00:03:18.885 --> 00:03:22.845
components of LingQ on the web were
completely rewritten from scratch,

00:03:22.845 --> 00:03:24.345
which we haven't done in a long time.

00:03:24.345 --> 00:03:29.595
And, and both the mobile apps were also
more or less rewritten from scratch.

00:03:29.595 --> 00:03:33.615
So, uh, significantly different than
when we went from version three to

00:03:33.615 --> 00:03:38.205
version four, uh, and, and a big part
of the reason why was we, we wanted

00:03:38.205 --> 00:03:43.010
to have a more stable platform that,
uh, that was easier to develop upon.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.459
I mean, the fact is that LingQ's been
around a long time and we've had a lot

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:54.120
of versions and, uh, you know, as you
evolve and iterate there ends up, it

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.519
ends up being tougher and tougher to,
to improve upon what's there before.

00:03:59.385 --> 00:04:01.785
Things have been built on top
of other things, which were

00:04:01.785 --> 00:04:03.135
built on top of other things.

00:04:03.135 --> 00:04:07.095
And so at a certain point, we
needed to have a fresh start.

00:04:07.515 --> 00:04:12.915
Um, when we then went to do that, it gave
us an opportunity to look at a lot of the

00:04:12.915 --> 00:04:17.475
things that we have done over the years,
uh, and to try and kind of streamline

00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:24.345
things and, um, make things clear, both
functionality wise and in the way that

00:04:24.855 --> 00:04:27.164
some of the things work on the site.

00:04:27.164 --> 00:04:29.775
So I know that one of the things
you're quite excited about these

00:04:29.775 --> 00:04:35.685
days is the coins, uh, and how
are our goal for maintaining your

00:04:35.685 --> 00:04:40.344
streak is no longer measured, uh,
or set in, in terms of LingQs.

00:04:40.365 --> 00:04:44.474
But in fact, set in terms of
coins, you can earn coins by

00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:51.755
creating LingQs, moving words up in
status, um, reading and listening.

00:04:51.755 --> 00:04:55.865
So there, there are many more ways of
earning coins and, and so that, uh,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:57.565
well I'll, maybe I'll let you continue.

00:04:57.565 --> 00:05:01.445
Well I mean any of you who have followed
my YouTube channel you know that I often

00:05:01.445 --> 00:05:06.445
use this metaphor of the, uh, racing
dogs who chase a mechanical rabbit.

00:05:07.015 --> 00:05:11.190
And anytime you can create
something that people are keen

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.659
on doing for whatever reason...

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.210
uh, so they're chasing this thing.

00:05:15.539 --> 00:05:17.719
And as a result, they're learning.

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:22.365
So uh, to me having coins, which you
can't, you can't buy anything at the

00:05:22.365 --> 00:05:25.695
grocery store with the coins, but
these are points that accumulate.

00:05:25.905 --> 00:05:30.594
And in the past, you know, if I was only
getting credit for say creating LingQs,

00:05:31.094 --> 00:05:34.545
but I was going through a period where I
wasn't creating new LingQs because I was

00:05:34.545 --> 00:05:39.135
reviewing old material, or I was doing
a lot of lists, or I was moving some of

00:05:39.135 --> 00:05:41.835
my status two or three words to known.

00:05:42.284 --> 00:05:44.115
Uh, I was getting no credit for that.

00:05:44.145 --> 00:05:49.545
Whereas now it's sort of merged into
one single unit that we follow coins.

00:05:49.935 --> 00:05:55.544
And so if I had, as I have now done,
uh, I'll go into my vocabulary list and

00:05:55.544 --> 00:05:59.945
I will select out status three words
and I'll move a lot of them to known.

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:02.085
And I get lots of coins for doing that.

00:06:02.505 --> 00:06:06.615
And whereas I'm normally washing
the dishes and listening now I'm

00:06:06.615 --> 00:06:09.165
also getting coins for listening.

00:06:09.585 --> 00:06:13.395
And in fact, I don't know all
the details, but a wider range

00:06:13.395 --> 00:06:15.495
of activities generate coins.

00:06:15.645 --> 00:06:19.035
And so you follow your progress
and your activity level, but

00:06:19.035 --> 00:06:22.365
it's not limited to just a couple
of things like creating LingQs.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:25.560
And I find that strangely motivating.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.460
The coins is one area where we
try to, to make things easier

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:30.690
to understand for people.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.690
Um, you know, in, in the past
we've had the activity score.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.620
We've had the activity apple,
we had LingQs target for

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.120
your streak, we had coins.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:47.715
So, as I say, in terms of simplification,
what we've done is we've, we've now

00:06:47.715 --> 00:06:51.165
gotten rid of the activity score,
which used to be the measure of how

00:06:51.165 --> 00:06:53.685
active you were for the last 30 days.

00:06:54.165 --> 00:06:58.455
Uh, and that determined the color of
your activity apple, uh, we've done away

00:06:58.455 --> 00:07:01.545
with the apple and that activity score.

00:07:01.545 --> 00:07:03.975
And instead we're, we're
just gonna use coins.

00:07:03.975 --> 00:07:05.295
So we're again, trying to simplify.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.750
Based on the number of coins you've
earned in the last 30 days, your

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:14.310
streak indicator will also change color
like the activity apple did before.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:20.310
So just a small example of, of a place
where we tried to kind of make things

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:25.170
simpler because the reality is for
new users, there was a lot to learn.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:29.580
And, and so, yeah, we, you know,
the danger in software is always to

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.390
keep building stuff and new features
and feature creep, as they say.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:39.540
And so here, we're trying to, trying
to declutter a bit, um, make things

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.430
less intimidating for new users.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:49.125
And at the same time, trying to, as you
say, have more activities count towards

00:07:49.215 --> 00:07:54.015
exactly the measure that, that, that,
that you're trying to move the needle on.

00:07:54.375 --> 00:07:57.945
I mean, it's, to me it's so important
to, to be active with the language.

00:07:58.185 --> 00:07:59.445
That's the number one thing.

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:03.555
I mean, we will progress more quickly
at some times, or for some languages

00:08:03.555 --> 00:08:07.695
than with others, but if we're, if we're
active, we're going to get somewhere and

00:08:07.785 --> 00:08:12.105
enabling, sort of, encouraging us to be
engaged in a number of different kinds

00:08:12.105 --> 00:08:16.485
of activities, and we'll see later how
it's become easier now to do a variety

00:08:16.485 --> 00:08:19.965
of things where you are engaged with the
language, moving forward in the language,

00:08:20.145 --> 00:08:22.605
getting credit for this and your coins.

00:08:22.875 --> 00:08:26.505
And I think that's going to motivate
more people and keep them more

00:08:26.505 --> 00:08:28.965
active with sort of less frustration.

00:08:28.965 --> 00:08:30.615
So I'm quite excited about that.

00:08:30.615 --> 00:08:34.695
We've also made some changes to the
way we track listening and reading.

00:08:35.025 --> 00:08:37.155
Um, so we're no longer...

00:08:38.145 --> 00:08:42.525
it used to be that you had to finish
a, a reading, a lesson, and then you

00:08:42.525 --> 00:08:44.385
got credit for having read it one time.

00:08:44.535 --> 00:08:47.715
And just like when you were listening
to the audio, you got credit.

00:08:48.435 --> 00:08:51.855
If, and when the audio file
finished playing, you got credit

00:08:51.855 --> 00:08:53.295
for having listened to at one time.

00:08:53.865 --> 00:08:55.755
Whereas now we're tracking in real time.

00:08:55.755 --> 00:08:58.575
So, so anytime you listen,
it's being tracked.

00:08:58.575 --> 00:09:01.495
And if you listen for 30 seconds,
then you'll see 30 seconds added

00:09:01.515 --> 00:09:03.945
to your listening, uh, right away.

00:09:05.375 --> 00:09:09.095
You know, obviously when you're
measuring coins for your daily streak,

00:09:09.095 --> 00:09:12.665
there's no point giving you credit
for listening to, uh, uh, or reading

00:09:12.665 --> 00:09:14.165
a lesson when it takes you a week.

00:09:14.165 --> 00:09:17.075
And we give you credit for having read
it on the seventh day that doesn't

00:09:17.075 --> 00:09:18.815
help you on the previous six days.

00:09:18.885 --> 00:09:22.775
Uh, it doesn't contribute
to your coins goal.

00:09:23.045 --> 00:09:25.475
So we track in real time now.

00:09:25.745 --> 00:09:31.035
One thing that has improved is the
ability to time-stamp in sentence mode.

00:09:31.515 --> 00:09:34.695
And so I've been listening to
this, uh, you know, in sentence

00:09:34.695 --> 00:09:39.825
mode, this Jordanian, uh, you know,
Levantine Arabic cartoon series.

00:09:40.395 --> 00:09:44.235
And very often, you know, it just
seems like they cut off, you know,

00:09:44.235 --> 00:09:47.205
at the beginning of the, whatever
it was, it seems like it should

00:09:47.205 --> 00:09:48.705
have started half a second earlier.

00:09:48.705 --> 00:09:52.515
It should have gone on a half a
second later or two a second later.

00:09:53.319 --> 00:09:56.650
Uh, every time I listen to it, I'm
listening to it over and over again,

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:01.090
in order to capture just when that
particular audio that corresponds to

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:05.439
that sentence, you know, what is the
actual audio snippet that I want there?

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:07.840
And every time I listen, I'm listening.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:13.060
And so, again, it's this idea that I'm
sort of induced to, to get involved in

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:17.355
these other activities, for whatever
reason, because I want to capture the

00:10:17.385 --> 00:10:21.855
accurate sound file, which by the way,
anyone who comes after me will benefit

00:10:21.855 --> 00:10:25.545
from, but as I'm doing that, I listen to
it three or four times, I'm listening.

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:29.595
And so it's just another example of how
we do things for a variety of reasons.

00:10:29.595 --> 00:10:32.955
But the net effect is we're more active
in the language and we're learning.

00:10:33.345 --> 00:10:39.705
And the fact of the matter is changes that
we made related to timestamping uh, here,

00:10:39.705 --> 00:10:42.195
we're talking about editing a sentence.

00:10:42.195 --> 00:10:45.225
So when you're in sentence mode,
there's an option to edit the sentence.

00:10:45.765 --> 00:10:50.285
Uh, that in itself I think
is a significant improvement.

00:10:50.375 --> 00:10:54.615
And maybe particularly for like,
I'm studying Japanese right now so

00:10:54.615 --> 00:10:57.795
particularly when you're studying
Japanese, you, you very often want to

00:10:57.795 --> 00:11:03.330
edit incorrect word splits uh, something
that can't be helped in the end.

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.540
Um, so it's easy.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:09.089
Edit that sentence, make a quick change,
save that sentence, keep LingQing.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:13.140
Whereas before you had to edit the
lesson, wait for that page to load,

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:18.120
find the spot in the text where you
want to fix that, uh, split, save it,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.410
make your way back to where you were.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.410
Uh, so that's, a small change that I
think will have a significant impact.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:29.660
And there, again, it allows us to
provide a better system, once we've

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:33.319
made the edit, a better system of
timestamping, which has many benefits.

00:11:33.319 --> 00:11:36.589
And that was something that for years,
people were asking for "hey, and you

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:41.750
have the, the text match the audio
file, move along with the audio in

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.380
some way so that we can follow along
and, and obviously to do that is a

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:47.150
lot of effort requires timestamping.

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.360
Now in the meantime, technology has
come a long way where a lot of the

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:54.770
time, now we can either import the
timestamps from services like YouTube,

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.070
or there are tools that will...

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.280
compare the audio file with the
text and automatically timestamp.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:05.115
And we've implemented some of those
uh, in the new version as well.

00:12:05.115 --> 00:12:11.955
And then these tools then allow, allow
you to fine tune those timestamps or

00:12:11.955 --> 00:12:15.795
in some cases, some languages, Japan,
Japanese, as an example, some languages

00:12:15.825 --> 00:12:20.895
don't have the automated timestamping
so there at least give you a better

00:12:21.285 --> 00:12:26.055
tool for manually timestamping lessons
than some, some users, some content

00:12:26.055 --> 00:12:27.355
providers will want to do that.

00:12:28.635 --> 00:12:31.815
Just an improvement and it, what
it, what it does is it lets us...

00:12:32.715 --> 00:12:38.715
for example, obviously more easily play
that audio snippet in sentence mode.

00:12:38.715 --> 00:12:41.415
So instead of text to speech
audio in sentence mode, you're

00:12:41.415 --> 00:12:45.915
getting that timestamp segment
from the actual audio file.

00:12:46.485 --> 00:12:50.515
And then, then one of the major new
innovations, as well as this listening

00:12:50.535 --> 00:12:55.215
mode, which is, uh, an audio playing
mode, where if you, if you click the audio

00:12:55.215 --> 00:12:59.834
player, there's an option in the audio
player to expand that view and, and, uh,

00:13:00.614 --> 00:13:02.220
see the listening mode, which is very...

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:07.950
functions, very similarly to a lyrics
mode and a music app like Apple Music

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:12.600
or Spotify so that you'll see the
text of what you're listening to.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.990
And the text, if the timestamping
is correct, the text will actually

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.820
highlight and move along as you listen.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:21.540
So those are significant improvements,
all kind of related to this

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.120
timestamping sentenced editing.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:29.635
A lot of these changes that we've
made all kind of impact in many ways.

00:13:29.845 --> 00:13:33.355
There's lots of fun things that like, I
discovered that in sentence mode, which

00:13:33.355 --> 00:13:36.925
I use a lot, especially in Persian and
Arabic, which I still find very difficult

00:13:36.925 --> 00:13:41.995
to read lo and behold, someone has gone
in there and put translations in...

00:13:42.525 --> 00:13:46.675
I was doing this Arabic series,
in Korean and Turkish, which are

00:13:46.675 --> 00:13:48.355
both languages that I have studied.

00:13:48.685 --> 00:13:52.255
So I not only hear this in Arabic and
then I see the English translation.

00:13:52.255 --> 00:13:55.075
I, if I'm interested, I can
also turn them off, but I can

00:13:55.075 --> 00:13:58.305
see uh, Korean and the Turkish.

00:13:58.335 --> 00:14:00.314
Now, if someone puts in a
French translation, I'll

00:14:00.314 --> 00:14:01.155
see the French translation.

00:14:01.275 --> 00:14:04.335
Well, the reality is you'll only see
those, if you edit that sentence.

00:14:04.545 --> 00:14:05.745
Oh, well, how did I see that?

00:14:05.745 --> 00:14:06.045
Anyway?

00:14:06.075 --> 00:14:07.155
Cause you edited the sentence.

00:14:07.875 --> 00:14:09.224
If you're, if you're...

00:14:09.935 --> 00:14:14.265
I believe dictionary language is set to
Turkish, then you'll see the Turkish.

00:14:14.805 --> 00:14:15.475
Oh, I see.

00:14:15.495 --> 00:14:15.855
Okay.

00:14:15.885 --> 00:14:18.645
Anyway, I saw three of them
anyway, surprises, whatever.

00:14:19.005 --> 00:14:19.425
Okay.

00:14:19.455 --> 00:14:20.355
What else have you got?

00:14:20.355 --> 00:14:26.715
The first thing that will strike people
when they open LingQ five is, is, um, that

00:14:26.715 --> 00:14:28.365
the library looks considerably different.

00:14:28.365 --> 00:14:33.015
And we, we actually felt that
the old library was in a bit of

00:14:33.015 --> 00:14:34.935
a weak point for version four.

00:14:34.965 --> 00:14:39.315
Uh, we, we didn't do a great job or at
least in our new version, we feel like

00:14:39.315 --> 00:14:46.560
we do a much better job of surfacing
the content, more, better content, uh,

00:14:46.569 --> 00:14:51.030
to keep people engaged, to help people
make sure they, to make sure people

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:54.150
can, can find content more easily.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:15:00.449
Um, finding content they like, and we
have, uh, we have far more, uh, links

00:15:00.449 --> 00:15:05.490
to external sources of content because
obviously the importing feature, which

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:12.735
is a very powerful, uh, component of,
of LingQ is, is great if you know where

00:15:12.735 --> 00:15:16.775
to find content, but it's not always
that easy, especially if, if you know,

00:15:16.775 --> 00:15:19.065
you're just starting out or, or yeah...

00:15:19.465 --> 00:15:26.115
the ability to, for us to source content
is just much greater than for individuals.

00:15:26.645 --> 00:15:31.155
We have, not that we're doing it
ourselves, I mean, many members of

00:15:31.155 --> 00:15:34.785
our community are great at sourcing
content and sharing that information

00:15:35.055 --> 00:15:36.765
on the forum with us, obviously.

00:15:37.785 --> 00:15:42.285
Uh, we have our volunteer volunteer
librarians, and we have our, of course

00:15:42.285 --> 00:15:44.864
our own internal content people.

00:15:44.864 --> 00:15:50.744
And everybody together is working hard
to find the best content out there so

00:15:50.744 --> 00:15:56.025
that we can present it in our library
and our new format, um, which...

00:15:58.725 --> 00:16:03.494
we feel looks a lot nicer and presents
things a lot better and, and then

00:16:03.494 --> 00:16:08.694
also has much more variety of content.

00:16:08.694 --> 00:16:12.415
And, and then more of these
external sources of content.

00:16:13.165 --> 00:16:14.994
There are some issues we've discovered.

00:16:15.145 --> 00:16:20.604
We had a long beta, you know, a QA testing
period, but there's so many different

00:16:20.604 --> 00:16:25.224
browsers and monitor sizes that there
have been some issues, uh, which we're

00:16:25.224 --> 00:16:31.260
going to go about resolving and in the
lesson page, uh, and you know, again,

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.860
what makes me excited is we've rewritten
it all as Mark says, and we have a

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:41.220
platform now where we can not only twig
any issues that are, you know, have been

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:47.490
identified in our new, uh, LingQ 5.0, but
the ability now to develop further, uh,

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.200
with new ideas and taking advantage of,
uh, all the different things that are out

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:57.510
there that are making language learning
more fun, more motivating for people,

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:01.920
less resistance, less frustration, less
charge around trying to look for content.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:05.740
Um, yeah, it's exciting
for me it's exciting.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:07.310
Oh for sure.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:13.050
And I mean, a big focus for us was,
was trying to simplify with this

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:17.250
update, try to reduce the clutter,
make things less intimidating.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:21.420
There are, obviously there are users
who aren't that happy with some of

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.690
the changes because they were used to
the previous interface and some, some

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:27.450
elements of it were more convenient.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.900
Uh, there are other elements
that were less convenient.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:35.820
In the end I think, you know, one of
the, one of the big benefits when you

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:41.740
talk about improving things is, is
as we move forward, we'll just, we,

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:47.399
we intend to introduce more and more
customization so that people can set

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:51.449
up their library, set up their reader,
how, how they would like to see it.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:56.460
Uh, and, and so that it's no
longer a one size fits all.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:00.990
Uh, so more and more customization,
which I think should, should, uh,

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:07.970
hopefully resolve a lot of the, um, uh,
issues that some users are reporting.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:12.284
I think as people get in and they discover
things like, I mean, we could go on

00:18:12.284 --> 00:18:15.345
forever, like the multiple playlist, a
whole bunch of other things like that.

00:18:15.524 --> 00:18:18.675
I suspect we will get more and
more requests for customization.

00:18:19.095 --> 00:18:23.175
And I guess in the new platform, we'll
be better able to accommodate that.

00:18:23.205 --> 00:18:27.165
I mean, we get those requests all the
time and had them for version four too.

00:18:27.314 --> 00:18:31.845
And we could, we just felt like we can't
keep making those kinds of improvements

00:18:31.845 --> 00:18:35.925
in version four, uh, on that platform.

00:18:36.899 --> 00:18:40.229
Just too restrictive and
too, too time consuming.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:43.800
That was a big driver for
the changes that we made.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:46.530
And yeah, there are lots of other
things there, there, the, the

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:49.199
multiple playlists is something that
people have asked for forever, or

00:18:49.199 --> 00:18:51.810
at least a way to organize content.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:59.489
So now you can, you want to organize
your, um, songs into a songs playlist.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:04.139
Or, or whatever you want to do, uh,
you can, you can use the playlist

00:19:04.139 --> 00:19:08.030
to play the audio, but you can
also just use the playlist to

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:11.100
organize, uh, lessons and courses.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:11.669
It's up to you.

00:19:11.669 --> 00:19:15.149
But there, there you have a tool
for organizing your library.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:23.909
Um, in addition, even the dark mode,
light mode, um, something we've

00:19:23.909 --> 00:19:24.870
been asked for for a long time.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.060
So, so that is there.

00:19:28.215 --> 00:19:29.475
Uh, which, and looks great.

00:19:29.475 --> 00:19:31.755
I really liked it the
way the dark mode works.

00:19:32.445 --> 00:19:38.685
I have it set to automatically switch
based on our computer settings.

00:19:40.115 --> 00:19:43.510
The light mode is set to be on
during the day in dark mode at night.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.030
By the way, importing is a
lot easier in the new system.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.210
I do a lot of importing and I
find it's a lot more convenient,

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:50.370
lot more, more intuitive.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:53.320
You mean that the actual import page page?

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:55.420
Yeah, we tried to clean that up too.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:56.470
I think it's much simpler.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:20:02.000
I mean, we had a lot of functionality
built on top of functionality

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:04.360
in the old, in the old view.

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:07.890
One thing that's, that's not yet in
the web version, but is an iOS and...

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:12.300
I can't remember if it's on
Android yet or not, but will be on

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:17.580
all  platforms, and that is the,
uh, full text, text to speech.

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:23.010
Uh, so any lessons that you import that
have no audio, you will, you will be

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:27.845
able to generate at least a text to
speech, audio version of that lesson.

00:20:28.745 --> 00:20:30.435
So that'll be, that'll be great.

00:20:30.465 --> 00:20:36.485
Yeah besides that, I guess I, you know,
small things in the reader, there's that

00:20:36.655 --> 00:20:41.235
words pop up, which shows when you first
opened a lesson, or if you click away

00:20:41.564 --> 00:20:47.104
a word which has a, you shows a list of
all your blue words shows a list of...

00:20:47.655 --> 00:20:48.195
Oh, yeah.

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:49.070
There's all kinds of...

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:52.950
...of your, your LingQs and actually
shows you a list of all the words in the

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:54.990
lesson, which we've never shown before.

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:59.370
So there, you can actually see all the
known words that you've told us that you

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:01.080
know, that you never created LingQs for.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:05.160
So, so that was something that people
actually asked us for all the time.

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:05.430
Yeah.

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:06.360
That's very handy.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:08.360
...which we've never provided before.

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.900
So just interesting.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.070
And you can see, you know, what,
what, what words are there,

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:17.790
what statuses they have, which
words you, you told us you knew.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:18.690
Uh, yeah.

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:21.500
Anyway, there's lots of
little things there and...

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:23.700
I encourage people to use different...

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:25.830
now, especially since you get
credit for it, in terms of

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:27.900
coins, go to the vocab section.

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:31.290
You know, one of the things that I
like to do is go there and select

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:36.360
all my status three words, and I can
look at them by frequency so that

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:37.770
the most frequent words will show up.

00:21:38.595 --> 00:21:42.075
Or I can look at them in
alphabetical order, which even for

00:21:42.135 --> 00:21:45.015
Persian or Arabic, there was some
kind of an alphabetical order.

00:21:45.345 --> 00:21:48.615
And that has the advantage that you'll
see related words, words that have, you

00:21:48.615 --> 00:21:51.675
know, uh, the same prefix sort of thing.

00:21:51.705 --> 00:21:54.795
They'll show up 3, 4, 5, 6 words
that are related to each other.

00:21:55.005 --> 00:21:55.755
It all helps.

00:21:56.055 --> 00:22:00.355
And as you're doing this and maybe moves,
moving some words from status one to

00:22:00.355 --> 00:22:04.375
status two or status two to three, or to
known you're getting coins all the time.

00:22:04.675 --> 00:22:08.725
So I encourage people to really
explore all the things that are there.

00:22:09.025 --> 00:22:12.715
If you don't, if some of the
favored features that you find

00:22:12.715 --> 00:22:17.395
are not as convenient for you in
the new LingQ 0.5, let us know.

00:22:17.755 --> 00:22:19.675
But at the same time, please be patient.

00:22:19.675 --> 00:22:23.175
I think what we've done here is create
something with tremendous opportunity

00:22:23.175 --> 00:22:25.125
for development going forward.

00:22:25.185 --> 00:22:28.485
So please take the time to
explore it and let us know your...

00:22:28.665 --> 00:22:33.375
I mean, I think, uh, the reality
is that that we've been using,

00:22:34.215 --> 00:22:36.105
you know, LingQ five internally.

00:22:36.135 --> 00:22:37.415
Uh, you know, I don't know...

00:22:37.445 --> 00:22:38.475
It's been a year.

00:22:38.524 --> 00:22:46.095
I haven't used LingQ four for over
a year on iOS, on Android and web.

00:22:46.095 --> 00:22:47.015
I've Simply...

00:22:47.575 --> 00:22:48.125
it's just...

00:22:48.745 --> 00:22:49.215
Much better.

00:22:49.515 --> 00:22:51.360
I don't want to be in version four.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:55.080
And, and so, but, but obviously I
I've been exposed to it a lot longer

00:22:55.140 --> 00:23:00.520
and have used it longer, but there's,
there's, there's you end up in

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:04.670
version four, uh, and it just feels...

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:09.770
it's just less it's it's it's
it's version five is, is, is a

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:11.690
bigger, is a big step forward.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:13.850
And, and we know you're going to love it.

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:15.140
Like we do.

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:18.800
And look forward to obviously hearing
your comments and your thoughts on some

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:20.150
of the things we talked about today.

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:21.560
Very exciting time for us...

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:22.160
Absolutely.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.080
... here at LingQ and we're
really happy to have it launch

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:26.390
because it did take us a while.

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:32.905
But as, as Steve said, we're set up for
for further enhancement now and looking

00:23:32.905 --> 00:23:34.795
